WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.840
I want to start today with something a&nbsp;
little different and and very interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.280
and important in understanding a lot of the&nbsp;
structures that have gotten the politics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.680
the United States to where they are today.
We're going to look at a new poll from The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.600
New York Times, from New York Times with&nbsp;
Siena College. And it's not about who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.640
winning Biden or Trump. It's really about one&nbsp;
of the fundamental inequalities that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.440
in the United States. It's not income inequality,&nbsp;
which we'll talk about later today with our guest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.920
It is a poll that confirms something I've been&nbsp;
talking about for many years, which is that on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:43.840
the issues, the United States electorate, meaning&nbsp;
people, you, me, us, we are well to the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:50.560
compared to number one, what many people might&nbsp;
assume is the left right balance of the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:58.080
But maybe more importantly, the country is to the&nbsp;
left of where our elected representatives are. Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.640
elected representatives are far more conservative&nbsp;
than the makeup of the United States. We have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.400
country where if you go to individual people&nbsp;
and you ask them about policy, very important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.600
You ask about policy issues, policy questions.&nbsp;
You come away with the impression that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.640
country is quite, quite left, certainly more&nbsp;
left than many people are willing to admit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.160
But then you look at governors and you look&nbsp;
at state legislatures and you look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.040
makeup of the Senate and the makeup of corporate&nbsp;
media. And it's sort of seems like a center right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.880
country. But as I've told you before, we have&nbsp;
three to four different systems in this country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.360
that keep our representation well to the right&nbsp;
of the actual electorate. So let's not get ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.960
of ourselves. Let's go through it in pieces. This&nbsp;
is one of those types of stories which, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.720
it doesn't it's not a click bait story. It's not&nbsp;
going to get 500000 views on YouTube. But if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.520
don't understand this, you miss a lot of why the&nbsp;
country is the way it is. So it's really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.360
to do. First and foremost, the New York Times&nbsp;
poll asked likely voters about their positions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.320
on a variety of issues. And when you look at&nbsp;
the numbers, there is nothing center right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:13.200
about this country. For example, 72 percent of&nbsp;
the country wants a second two trillion dollar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.360
KORONA My current corona virus stimulus package,&nbsp;
72 percent. Only 21 percent oppose a second corona&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:26.640
virus stimulus in a country this polarized and&nbsp;
seemingly divided. Seventy two percent agreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.400
on anything is stunning. 72 percent want the&nbsp;
two trillion second stimulus. How about this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.440
67 percent of the country believes there should be&nbsp;
a public health insurance option. Now, I know it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.880
not single payer. It's not Medicare for all.&nbsp;
But remember when the debate started in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.280
2009 about the Affordable Care Act or Obamacare,&nbsp;
President Obama started the negotiation at me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.560
Know, I'd like a public option. The Republican&nbsp;
Party went crazy pretending it was some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.360
socialist government takeover of health care. And&nbsp;
yet 67 percent of this country agrees we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.120
we should have it. When you hear Trump talk&nbsp;
about we're going to get rid of Obamacare at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.800
rallies and his crowd cheers and Fox News talks&nbsp;
about it in less than glowing terms, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.920
get the impression that there's a lot of debate&nbsp;
among the public, but 67 percent of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:24.720
agrees. This assumption, something we should have&nbsp;
something seem is something this controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.800
with hundreds of millions of dollars spent&nbsp;
in opposition and two thirds of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.840
flat out says we should have we should have this&nbsp;
option. There should be such a government option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.360
Now it gets more interesting. Joe&nbsp;
Biden's two trillion dollar climate plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.920
which Donald Trump and Republicans present pretend&nbsp;
is crazy. They say it'll end the auto industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.280
They say it'll end fossil fuel industry.&nbsp;
They say it's outrageous. It's socialist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:00.640
Sixty six percent of the country supports&nbsp;
Joe Biden's two trillion dollar climate plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.680
which includes getting back into the Paris climate&nbsp;
accords. Some elements using the Green New Deal as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.840
a framework, phasing out fossil fuel vehicles on&nbsp;
and on and on. Two thirds of the country wants it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.880
even things that have become almost rallying cries&nbsp;
of the right, like their opposition to a national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.160
MASC mandate, which, by the way, would really&nbsp;
be a president saying, hey, governors, please&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.600
do mask mandates in your states. And it would&nbsp;
ultimately be up to governors whether to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.440
or not. Fifty nine percent of the country wants a&nbsp;
national MASC mandate. Here's another interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.040
one. Fracking. Donald Trump and Republicans have&nbsp;
made a huge deal out of saying Joe Biden will ban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:48.400
fracking. It's not actually what Biden would do.&nbsp;
Biden has a plan to phase it out over time. But as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:54.080
if continuing to frack is so important. Trump has&nbsp;
been hammering, hammering, hammering Biden on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.800
only 44 percent of the country wants fracking&nbsp;
to exist. And. Almost equal amount. 42 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.400
doesn't. And 14 percent isn't sure. A big portion&nbsp;
of that. I believe if they learn more about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.160
would would be would be against fracking. So&nbsp;
this is not a right wing country. When you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.800
at this, I mean, even if you just say, let's&nbsp;
only look at policies with 60 percent approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.240
or higher, 60 percent or higher support, that&nbsp;
would mean we get a two trillion dollar second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:28.880
coronavirus stimulus, public health insurance. And&nbsp;
Biden's two trillion dollar climate plan. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.360
just if we set the line at 60 percent and we say&nbsp;
only do things where 60 percent of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:42.800
agrees. That's three major policies. That would be&nbsp;
a major leftward switch. So we then must ask the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.960
question, why does the Senate, 53 Republicans?&nbsp;
Why do we have 26 Republican governors? Why is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.080
it so hard to remove a lunatic Republican&nbsp;
president? And as I've said before, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:00.160
systems that favor the American right. Example&nbsp;
number one, Electoral College. The Electoral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:07.200
College system has handed Republican wins to&nbsp;
Republican candidates who lost the popular vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:14.720
Much like on the issues, the will of the people&nbsp;
was to elect the Democrat. The Electoral College&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:21.760
handed it to a Republican. That's a systemic help&nbsp;
to the right example. Number two, gerrymandering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.160
which is the strategic drawing of congressional&nbsp;
districts, has allowed way more Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.440
members of the House than you would have with&nbsp;
fairly drawn districts. The Democratic Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.320
would have a bigger majority right&nbsp;
now were it not for gerrymandering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.240
There are previous Republican majorities,&nbsp;
which may have not been Republican majorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.800
were it not for gerrymandering, systemic help&nbsp;
to the American right. Example number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.960
the Senate. As an institution, when you give&nbsp;
each state to senators and the states vary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:59.920
widely in population, low population states get&nbsp;
disproportionately more power. California has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:06.400
60, 70 times the population of Wyoming. California&nbsp;
has two senators. Wyoming has two senators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:13.120
Wyoming is a red state. Wyoming. Senators have&nbsp;
insanely more power relative to their populations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:20.640
than California. Because conservatives prefer on&nbsp;
average to live more rurally, whereas Democrats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:28.000
left wingers, progressives prefer to live more&nbsp;
in urban or suburban settings at a systemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:35.440
level that gives more power to fewer right leaning&nbsp;
people. So the key takeaways here are not to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:39.520
tricked by really bad talking points for many&nbsp;
on the right, including talking points like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.240
this is a center right country. It's&nbsp;
not. When you fairly poll people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.960
on issues, you see that this is a center left to&nbsp;
left country. And number two, we have systemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:59.760
elements of how our system is organized that&nbsp;
have our representation be far to the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.440
of what the actual country believes&nbsp;
on a number of different issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.080
Something to keep in mind as we go forward over&nbsp;
the next two weeks towards this upcoming election.

